theory.intro:
A **composite figure** is made by joining simple shapes together. Its perimeter is the distance around the outside, so work out any unlabelled side before you add.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[dashed] (0,2) -- (2,2);
\draw[thick] (0,0) -- (4,0) -- (4,2) -- (2,2) -- (2,4) -- (0,4) -- cycle;
\node[above] at (1,2.05) {join};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
**Push-out shortcut:** for an L-shape or a staircase whose steps run in one direction, the step sides push out into a rectangle without changing their total length, so $P = 2 \times (\text{overall width} + \text{overall height})$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[dashed] (3,4) -- (5,4) -- (5,2);
\draw[thick] (0,0) -- (5,0) -- (5,2) -- (3,2) -- (3,4) -- (0,4) -- cycle;
\node[below] at (2.5,-0.2) {overall width};
\node[rotate=90] at (-0.6,2) {overall height};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

An inward corner alone does not rule out the shortcut: L-shapes and T-shapes can have the same perimeter as their enclosing rectangle. For a rectangular indentation cut into a rectangle's boundary, as shown below, the indentation replaces a straight section with an equal width plus two extra sides of depth $d$, adding $2d$ to the rectangle's perimeter.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[dashed] (2,4) -- (4,4);
\draw[thick] (0,0) -- (6,0) -- (6,4) -- (4,4) -- (4,2) node[midway, left] {$d$} -- (2,2) -- (2,4) node[midway, right] {$d$} -- (0,4) -- cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

